				<div class="info">
					<span class="printuser avatarhover"><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;"  ><img class="small" src="http://www.wikidot.com/avatar.php?userid=1918720&amp;amp;size=small&amp;amp;timestamp=1608407251" alt="Scorpion451" style="background-image:url(http://www.wikidot.com/userkarma.php?u=1918720)"/></a><a href="http://www.wikidot.com/user:info/scorpion451" onclick="WIKIDOT.page.listeners.userInfo(1918720); return false;" >Scorpion451</a></span> <span class="odate time_1608400041 format_%25e%20%25b%20%25Y%2C%20%25H%3A%25M%7Cagohover">19 Dec 2020 17:47</span>
																	<br/>
						in discussion <a href="/forum/c-88827/foundation-universe">SCP Universe / Foundation Universe</a> &raquo;
						<a href="/forum/t-13803170/architecture-of-sites-and-areas">Architecture of sites and areas</a>
														</div>
			</div>
			<div class="content" id="post-content-4848743">
									

<p>My headcanon is that you might have a lobby where the place looks a little better or a major campus-type site might have a fancy front office building for impressing politicians or representatives, but outside that sites are designed by engineers, not architects.</p>
<p>That is, the office and light lab areas have that look where you can swap hallways between any generic office, academic campus, research facility, government building or hospital by changing the colors of the walls and tile. You can furnish your office, cubicle, and/or quarters as you like (within reason), and there's obviously going to be human touches like plants in empty corners and parrot streamers left up in the lounge from last year's luau-themed staff cookout, but for the structure itself, the key word is &quot;pragmatic&quot;. Cubicle farms and copy-paste design are the rule, and you wouldn't see much difference between low-security humanoid containment cells and quarters for resident staff.</p>
Hardened lab and high security areas drop even that much accommodation to comfort-
<div class="collapsible-block">
<div class="collapsible-block-folded"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">+&nbsp;and&nbsp;are&nbsp;where&nbsp;the&nbsp;concept-development&nbsp;side&nbsp;of&nbsp;my&nbsp;brain&nbsp;really&nbsp;kicks&nbsp;in.</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded" style="display:none">
<div class="collapsible-block-unfolded-link"><a class="collapsible-block-link" href="javascript:;">-&nbsp;Cue&nbsp;Danny&nbsp;Elfman&nbsp;Music:</a></div>
<div class="collapsible-block-content">
<p>The standard architecture for a mass storage site is a three dimensional grid of reinforced columns, bulkheads, and crossmembers, spaced with redundant support capacity in expectation that sooner or later something is going breach and take a bunch of them with it. Concrete, refractory brick, and powder-painted steel are the materials of choice; convoluted networks of pipes and cables line the walls and ceilings. These areas tend to be underground with floorspace most relatably measured in city blocks.</p>
<p>Need a few humanoid containment chambers? Fill in a cube of support columns, add subdivisions, doors and catwalks as needed. Need a bigger room? Tear down a wall or two, annex a section of hallway.</p>
<p>Sites of this sort almost inevitably become winding mazes of hallways threaded between chambers, with near constant construction and reconstruction going on as new anomalies are contained and the needs of existing ones shift. Following pipes marked with yellow triangles or green &quot;5&quot;s is often the most reliable means of navigation, and periodic reviews are done to separate genuine reports of structural anomalies from hallways that disappeared due to renovation.</p>
</div>
</div>
</div>

